    "name": "AI Security",
    "slug": "ai-security",
    "count": 43,
    "guide": "AI Security is the discipline of protecting the AI systems themselves, not just the software they run inside. The day to day work centers on the model as the attack surface, things like prompt injection, jailbreaks that trick a model into ignoring its instructions, model theft or extraction, training data poisoning, and adversarial inputs designed to fool a classifier or an agent. Postings in our data include titles like Security Researcher for Agentic AI Threats, AI Security Research and Red Team Engineer, and dedicated red team research roles at frontier labs and government-adjacent AI safety institutes. Much of the work is adversarial by design, probing a model's own behavior rather than the servers around it. Red-teaming an AI system means trying to make the model itself misbehave, not scanning a network for open ports.\n\nThis is where people confuse AI Security with Application and Product Security, and with general Offensive Security, and the boundary matters. Application and Product Security protects the software wrapper around a model, the APIs, authentication, data storage, and CI/CD pipeline, treating the AI feature as one more component to secure like any other. Offensive Security and traditional red teaming target networks, applications, and infrastructure broadly, using penetration testing and exploit development against conventional attack surfaces. AI Security is narrower and stranger than both, because the vulnerability often lives in the model's weights, its training data, or its reasoning process rather than in a line of exploitable code. A few postings blend these worlds on purpose, such as Notion's AI Product Security role or Replit's AI Agent Security Architect, but the specialist core of AI Security is still about treating the AI system as the thing under attack.\n\nCredentials are thin here because this is a genuinely emerging field, and we will not pretend otherwise. None of the postings in our data name a dedicated AI security certification, because one has not become standard yet. What substitutes in practice is a general security credential or background, often paired with hands-on machine learning experience or an ML-adjacent degree, since employers are hiring for the blend rather than a single certificate. If you are building toward this field, invest in provable security fundamentals and real exposure to how models are trained and deployed, since that combination is what the postings are actually asking for.\n\nExperience requirements skew toward the middle and upper end of the ladder in our data. Mid-level is the single most common seniority tag, but Senior, Staff, Principal, Director, Lead, and Manager titles appear repeatedly, especially at companies building or securing frontier models. True entry-level AI Security postings are rare in what we tracked, which lines up with the field's blended nature, employers seem to want candidates who already have either a security foundation or an ML foundation before they specialize further. The path that gets people hired most often looks like a security engineer picking up applied ML skills, or a machine learning engineer picking up security discipline, rather than someone starting from zero in both.\n\nOn technical skills, the postings we pulled point clearly at Large Language Models, Python, and Rust as the most common technical threads, alongside cloud platforms AWS, Azure, and GCP and container tooling like Kubernetes. Security-specific skills that recur include Red Teaming, Threat Modeling, Penetration Testing, and NIST framework familiarity, alongside ML tooling such as PyTorch, TensorFlow, LangChain, and Hugging Face for roles closer to the model layer. Governance-flavored postings add SOC 2, ISO 27001, and GDPR into the mix, reflecting how much of enterprise AI security work is compliance-adjacent as well as technical.\n\nOn today's market, we currently track approximately 52 open AI Security postings live in our database. Of the 112 postings we pulled for this analysis, 54 listed usable numeric salary data, ranging from about 64,500 dollars to roughly 282,680 dollars a year, a wide spread that reflects everything from cleared government contractor roles to principal-level positions at major technology companies. Booz Allen Hamilton appears most often in our data by a clear margin, reflecting the volume of cleared AI-adjacent roles the defense contracting sector is posting right now. ServiceNow, Anthropic, and Elastic also show up repeatedly, alongside Citi's dedicated AI Offensive Security Analyst postings. Worth noting honestly, a good share of the government-contractor listings are broader AI or machine learning roles that simply require a security clearance, rather than pure AI-security specialist work, so read that segment of the market with that context in mind.",
